<section id="intro"><h2>1. Introduction</h2>
<p>In Systems Engineering at Universidad del Pacífico, documenting continuous processes is essential. In English, the <em class="pp">Present Perfect Progressive</em> (<i>have/has + been + verb-ing</i>) expresses these ongoing actions, connecting past effort with present results. Throughout our studies in Buenaventura, my team and I have been developing a web-based platform called SICUA. For months, we have been analyzing access vulnerabilities and have been designing digital validation tools for campus security. Mastering this structure allows us to communicate our technical progress effectively in international environments and developer portfolios.</p>
</section>

<section id="why"><h2>2. Justification</h2>
<div class="grid"><div class="box"><h3>Pedagogical</h3><p>The Present Perfect Progressive does not have a direct, one-to-one literal translation into Spanish. By applying it directly to our real-life work as software developers, learning grammar stops being just a mechanical exercise and turns into something genuinely meaningful, helping us describe our actual workflow naturally.</p></div><div class="box"><h3>Professional</h3><p>This tense is a must-have for our careers. We use it when preparing for technical interviews in English, writing commit histories or documentation on GitHub, and sharing code progress on platforms like Stack Overflow with phrases like “I have been developing...”</p></div><div class="box"><h3>Local</h3><p>Coming from Buenaventura, building SICUA lets us tell our own story. It highlights how we tackle local technological gaps and connectivity hurdles through continuous self-taught engineering, turning regional challenges into digital solutions.</p></div></div></section>

<section id="goals"><h2>3. Objectives</h2>
<h3>General objective</h3>
<p>To demonstrate communicative competence through a digital portfolio (DevLife Log) narrating our academic process in the Systems Engineering program using the Present Perfect Progressive tense.</p>
<h3>Specific objectives</h3>
<ol class="s"><li>To differentiate the Present Perfect Progressive structure from the simple present and continuous tenses within software development contexts.</li><li>To research and integrate specialized technical vocabulary in gerund (-ing) related to web development, databases, and system security.</li><li>To produce oral and written texts detailing our professional trajectory and the development phases of the SICUA project.</li><li>To strengthen technical storytelling for future professional profiles and international job interviews.</li></ol></section>

<section id="proof"><h2>4. Learning achievements and goals</h2>
<h3>Linguistic: 15 sentences in Present Perfect Progressive</h3>
<ol class="s"><li>I <b>have been developing</b> the SICUA authentication module since the beginning of the academic term.</li><li>My partners <b>have been designing</b> database schemas all week.</li><li>We <b>have been testing</b> the guard interface recently to prevent bottlenecks.</li><li>The university <b>has been lacking</b> digital access registries for years.</li><li>I <b>have been studying</b> software engineering principles lately.</li><li>Administrators <b>have been managing</b> user roles since the project's inception.</li><li>We <b>have been configuring</b> Flask routes all month.</li><li>Guards <b>have been validating</b> prototype codes recently.</li><li>Students <b>have been requesting</b> better campus security for a long time.</li><li>I <b>have been debugging</b> backend scripts all morning.</li><li>The team <b>has been integrating</b> event visitor registries recently.</li><li>We <b>have been optimizing</b> mobile web views since last week.</li><li>Our professor <b>has been evaluating</b> our progress all semester.</li><li>I <b>have been writing</b> technical documentation lately.</li><li>We <b>have been building</b> scalable web solutions for months.</li></ol>
<h3>Technical-communicative</h3>
<p>My team and I have been working on SICUA for the past few months. We have been using Python and Flask for the backend, MySQL for the database, and HTML and CSS for the interface. We have been building features for each user role, a guard interface to validate access codes, and an event module for external visitors.</p>
<h3>Product</h3>
<p>This DevLife Log is our final digital product, published as a portfolio and written 100% in English. We have been organizing our project information and our progress in this document to show our learning process.</p>
<h3>Attitudinal: self-evaluation</h3>
<p>We have been reflecting on our progress as Systems Engineering students. We have been identifying our strengths, such as teamwork and backend development, and the areas we need to improve, such as speaking English without reading.</p>
